.TH CHTTPCLIENT 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
chttpclient \- overview of the HTTP/1.1 client of the C Collections Library
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
chttpclient sends HTTP/1.1 requests and reads the responses, over plain
TCP, TLS or a Unix domain socket. chttpclient.h includes chttp.h, which
holds the types that the client shares with the server:
  - chttp_method_t.
  - chttp_request_body_t and its CHTTP_*_BODY macros.
  - chttp_tls_config_t.
  - The CHTTP_STATUS_* constants.
  - The base64 helpers and the Basic auth helpers.

Link a program that uses the client with \-lccollections. The library
itself depends on OpenSSL (libssl and libcrypto), so a link against the
shared library needs nothing more. For a static link, get the full list
from pkg-config:

    cc app.c $(pkg-config --cflags --libs --static ccollections)

You can build the library without the client (make
WITH_CHTTPCLIENT=0); a program that uses the client then does not link.
.fi
.SS The client handle
.nf
chttpcli is an opaque VALUE handle: a uint64_t, not a pointer. Do not
cast it to or from a pointer, and do not use it as an address.
CHTTPCLI_INVALID is 0 and is the chttpcli equivalent of NULL, so
"if (!cli)" tests whether there is a client.

Each call resolves the handle before it uses the client, so it finds
and refuses a stale handle (a handle whose client was destroyed). A
stale handle gives:
  - ccol_invalid_args from a function.
  - NULL from a function that returns a future.
  - A fatal error from chttpclient_destroy.
A stale handle never causes a read of freed memory.

ccol_create_chttpclient and ccol_create_chttpclient_mp make a client,
and chttpcli_construct, chttpcli_construct_scoped, chttpcli_declare and
chttpcli_declare_scoped are wrappers around them. chttpclient_destroy
destroys a client.

chttp_default_client gives one client for the whole process, which
chttp_get, chttp_post, chttp_put, chttp_delete, chttp_patch, chttp_do
and chttp_run_query use. The library owns that client; do not destroy
it.
.fi
.SS Requests
.nf
A chttp_request_t holds a method, a URL, a body and a set of headers.
chttp_request_new and chttp_request_new_mp copy the URL and the body,
so the caller can free its own buffers immediately. The fields method,
url, body, expect_continue and prevent_tls_downgrade_on_redirect are
public; use chttp_request_set_header and chttp_request_get_header to
set and read the headers. chttp_request_free frees the request.
.fi
.SS Responses
.nf
chttpcli_response has four public fields:

  status_code  the HTTP status of the final response.
  body         a heap buffer that ends with a NUL, or NULL.
  body_len     the number of bytes in body before that NUL.
  headers      a chmap(char * -> char *) from each lower-case field
               name to its value.

body is NULL and body_len is 0 in these cases:
  - A body of zero length, for example a 204, a response to HEAD or a
    "Content-Length: 0".
  - All streaming requests, whose body goes to the write callback.
So before you read body, make sure that body != NULL or body_len > 0,
even after a success.

When the server sends a name on more than one line, that name maps to
the values joined with ", ". Set-Cookie is the exception: it maps to
its first value. See chttpclient_resp_header(3). chttpclient_resp_free
frees the response. A response does not depend on the client that made
it, so you can free it before or after chttpclient_destroy.

Each entry point that takes a resp_out sets *resp_out to NULL before it
does other work, and keeps it NULL on each return that is not a
success. Because chttpclient_resp_free(NULL) does nothing, a call to it
after such a request is always safe.
.fi
.SS Three ways to run a request
.nf
Tier 1 is chttpclient_do and chttpclient_do_streaming. It runs the
request on the calling thread, which blocks until the request ends. The
pool size of the client (chttpclient_set_pool_size) sets the maximum
number of Tier 1 requests that run at the same time; a caller above
that maximum waits for a slot.

Tier 2 is chttpclient_do_async and chttpclient_do_async_streaming. It
gives the request to a shared reactor engine and returns a ctpool_future
immediately; chttpclient_async_result_get waits for the result.

Tier 3 is chttpclient_do_pooled and chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming. It
submits the request through the same engine and blocks until the
request ends, with calls of the same shape as those of Tier 1. Many
callers then share the few threads of the engine instead of each
request blocking a thread of its own.

Tiers 2 and 3 use one engine for each process, which all clients share.
It starts on first use and stops automatically when no request is in
progress and no connection is in the pool. Configure it with
chttpcli_set_engine_logger, chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs and
chttpcli_set_engine_num_reactor_threads. It is separate from the
reactor of chttpserver.

All three tiers apply the same rules to URLs, redirects, headers, TLS,
"Expect: 100-continue", interim responses and limits, and all three
give the same result codes.
.fi
.SS Connection reuse
.nf
After a response on a keep-alive connection, the client keeps that
connection in an idle pool. The key of the pool is the origin:
  - "scheme://host:port", where the host comparison ignores case.
  - "unix://<path>" for a Unix domain socket.
An HTTPS connection keeps its TLS session, so a later request to the
same origin does not do DNS, the TCP handshake or the TLS handshake.

The pool has a maximum for each origin and a maximum in total, and an
idle connection expires after a short time. Above a maximum, the client
closes a finished connection instead of putting it in the pool.

Before the client reuses a connection from the pool, it checks the
connection. If the peer closed the connection, or sent data on it while
it was idle, the client closes that connection and uses a different
one, at no cost to the caller. Bytes that arrive after a complete
response answer no request, and the client never reads them as the
response to the next request.

A reused connection can be dead, and the client can find this only
after it writes the request. If the client read nothing back on that
connection, it sends the request one more time, on a new connection.
The client never does this after it sent a body that a "100 Continue"
confirmed: the server accepted that body, and a second send could run
a request that is not idempotent two times.

The client never keeps these connections:
  - A connection whose response started before the client sent the
    full request.
  - A connection whose request had an "Expect: 100-continue" that got
    a final status as the answer.
  - A connection whose HTTP/1.0 response has Transfer-Encoding, which
    RFC 9112 section 6.1 calls faulty framing.
chttpclient_set_tls closes all idle HTTPS connections of the client,
because the pool key does not include the TLS settings of a handshake.
.fi
.SS Unix domain sockets
.nf
A URL of the form "http+unix://<percent-encoded socket path>/path?query"
reaches a server on a Unix domain socket. This works in all tiers and
with keep-alive pooling.

Percent-encode each byte of the socket path that is not an RFC 3986
unreserved character, '/' included. For example, "/run/app.sock"
becomes "%2Frun%2Fapp.sock".

The Host header is "localhost" by default, as with curl --unix-socket,
and a request can set its own Host header. A path that is too long for
sockaddr_un.sun_path gives ccol_http_invalid_url; on Linux, that limit
is 108 bytes, including the terminating NUL.

There is no "https+unix://", and chttpclient_set_tls has no effect on
such a request. A URL of this form has no credentials. A redirect can
never move a request to this transport, or from one socket to a
different socket; see chttpclient_do(3).
.fi
.SS Default request headers
.nf
The client adds a value for these headers:
  - Host.
  - Accept ("*/*").
  - User-Agent.
  - Content-Length and Content-Type, from the body.
  - Authorization, from credentials in the URL.
  - Expect, from expect_continue.
It does not add a header that the request sets (the name comparison
ignores case), and it sends a header that the request sets exactly as
given. Only POST, PUT and PATCH have a body; see
chttp_request_new_mp(3).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A TLS connection can renegotiate (TLS 1.2) or get a KeyUpdate
(TLS 1.3) during a request, and must then read before it can write, or
write before it can read. Each tier waits for the direction that the
connection asks for, so such a connection continues to make progress.

The client opens each socket close-on-exec, and also each file that
its TLS layer reads, so a process that the application starts (system,
popen, posix_spawn, fork and exec) gets no connection.

The client never changes the disposition of SIGPIPE, and none of its
writes causes SIGPIPE; see chttpclient_do(3).

There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with the client:
  - Call fork(2) before you create a client and before the first
    request of Tier 2 or Tier 3, so that each process makes its own
    clients.
  - Call fork(2) and then call exec(3) in the child immediately, as
    popen and system do. This is supported at all times, whatever the
    client does.
A child that continues with no exec(3) while a client from before the
fork is live is not supported, because fork(2) does not copy the
threads of the client and its engine.

A process can destroy all clients that used Tier 2 or Tier 3 before it
forks; the child of that process can then use both tiers, and starts
its own engine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Each function that takes a chttpcli is thread-safe: many threads can
run requests on one client, and change its settings, at the same time.

chttpclient_destroy can run while other threads use the client, and it
waits for the requests that are in progress. Two destroys of one handle
that overlap are a fatal error, so the application must serialize the
destroy, and the destroy must be the last thing that any thread does
with that handle.

A chttp_request_t and a chttpcli_response have no lock. A thread that
changes one of them must have exclusive access, and so must a thread
that reads one of them while a different thread changes it.
.fi
